  "query_preview": "..FOR EXTREME SOUTHWESTERN CALIFORNIA INCLUDING ORANGE...\nSAN DIEGO...WESTERN RIVERSIDE AND SOUTHWESTERN SAN BERNARDINO\nCOUNTIES...\n\nAreas of high clouds are being observed on satellite this morning. \nAreas of clouds will stick around through the afternoon, but coverage \nwill be much less widespread compared to yesterday. Night and \nmorning low clouds are expected to stay mostly confined to coastal \nareas this week, with patchy coverage.\n\nBroad ridging is still expected across the southwest this week. \nToday, the ridge is centered farther east, over New Mexico/Texas. \nThe ridge is expected to strengthen and expand westward Thursday and \nFriday settling over the Arizona/New Mexico border for Saturday into \nearly next week. High temperatures will start to become above \naverage on Wednesday, peaking at 5 to 10 degrees above average \nThursday through the weekend. In addition to above average high \ntemperatures, low temperatures will be running 5 to 10 degrees above \naverage, locally up to 15 degrees above average in the mountains. \nThe combination of elevated high and low temperatures will limit the \nability to recover from heat overnight and allow for conditions to \nquickly warm once the sun rises. Widespread moderate to locally \nmajor HeatRisk is expected in inland Orange County, San Diego \nValleys, Inland Empire, and area mountains. Major HeatRisk is \nexpected in the deserts, with locally extreme HeatRisk in Coachella \nValley and San Diego deserts. Major to extreme HeatRisk indicates \nthat anyone may be negatively impacted by the heat if the proper \nprecautions are not taken. \n\nWhile there will be a minor uptick in monsoonal moisture in the \nmountains and deserts Thursday and Friday, chances of showers and \nthunderstorms stay low. Shower chances are mostly 15 to 20 percent, \nwith only a 5 percent chance of thunderstorms developing. Dry \nconditions are expected over the weekend into early next week.",
